message: "If you use this work, please cite it as follows."
title: "Personal Data in Foundation Model Systems: Lifecycle Risks, Measurement, and Governance"
type: article
authors:
  - family-names: "Navaie"
    given-names: "Keivan"
    affiliation: "Lancaster University; The Alan Turing Institute; Machine Learning Theory and Alignment Scholars"
    email: "k.navaie@lancaster.ac.uk"
abstract: >
  Foundation models (FMs) routinely ingest and generate content that can contain personal data (PD), creating
  risks that span training, retrieval, and deployment. This survey systematizes the FM privacy surface, catalogs
  failure modes (memorization, extraction, inversion, membership inference, RAG/agent exfiltration), and evaluates
  mitigations across the lifecycle. It unifies data-centric controls, privacy-aware training, inference/output
  mediation, and post-training interventions into a defense-in-depth program, and distills deployable protocols
  and reporting guidance for canary exposure, targeted extraction/red-teaming, membership inference, and cross-
  snapshot leakage. The survey further maps regulatory and standards duties (e.g., GDPR, EU AI Act, NIST/ISO) to
  concrete controls, evidence artifacts, and KPIs, and provides deployment-oriented artifacts (red-team prompts,
  detector configs, DP recipes, RAG policy evals, DPIA templates, assurance scaffolds, telemetry schema) to
  support reproducible evaluation and deployment-grade assurance.
keywords:
  - foundation models
  - personal data
  - privacy
  - membership inference
  - exposure
  - retrieval-augmented generation
  - RAG
